WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.030
INTRO:
Netflix is officially the worst performing

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:10.530
stock in the S&amp;P 500 being down over 60%,
and that’s just since the start of the year.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:16.180
If we measure from their peak in late November,
we’ll see that Netflix is down nearly 70%.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:22.980
This means that you can get Netflix stock
for the same price as 4 years ago, and the

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:29.600
Netflix has shed an eye watering $216.8 billion
worth of market cap.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.190
This is quite a turn for Netflix given that
Netflix was the best performing stock of the

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:40.440
last decade growing over 4000% percent.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:49.399
Yet, currently, they’re on the other end
of the spectrum.

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:55.160
A few weeks ago, I made a similar video discussing
the state of Netflix, and at the time, they

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.620
were already doing quite bad being down over
50%.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.480
But, they were starting to make a bit of a
recovery, and it seemed like it was just a

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.110
matter of time until Netflix would recover
from the downtrend.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:11.850
Eversince Netflix announced that they lost
200,000 subscribers and warned that more subscriber

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.930
losses could be coming in the future, things
haven’t been looking as optimistic.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:21.380
It looks like this crash may be more of a
bubble popping event as opposed to a simple

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.470
market downturn.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.869
So, is Netflix past the point of recovery?

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:32.770
FUNDAMENTAL DOWNFALL:
The first thing to note is that we can no

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:36.040
longer just chaulk up this sell off to a market
downturn.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.590
While the market hasn’t been making new
all time highs week after week like it was

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.619
last year, it’s also not like its down 40
or 50%.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:49.020
The S&amp;P 500 is only down about 9% and the
Nasdaq is down 18%.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.560
Such a sell off may lead to a megacap like
Netflix selling off 30 maybe 40%, but not

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.159
70%.

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:57.680
Just look at Tesla for example.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.799
Tesla is likely the most richly valued growth
stock in the entire market, yet they’re

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:07.560
only down 19% from their all time high because
their still killing it fundamentally.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.880
So clearly, Netflix’s sell off has a lot
more to do with fundamental shortfalls as

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.830
opposed to market shortfalls.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:18.220
With that being said, the biggest challenge
ahead for Netflix is continuing subscriber

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.290
growth.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.690
Netflix and a bunch of their investors have
been trying to justify their recent subscriber

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.570
loss by blaming it on Russia.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:30.000
You see, Netflix had to write off 700,000
subscribers due to Russian account suspensions,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.480
so if we exclude this amount, Netflix would’ve
acutally gained 500,000 subscribers last quarter.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.910
But honestly, this doesn’t really change
anything.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.670
Even if we give Netflix the benefit, and say
that their subscribers grew by half a million

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.990
last quarter, compared to their 221 million
existing subscribers, this growth is absolute

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:49.020
garbage.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:54.280
In terms of percentages, this comes out to
just 0.226% growth.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:59.100
Even if we extrapolate this growth to an entire
year, we don’t even get 1% growth.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.940
For perspective, even mature companies like
Apple and Google generally post 20-30% growth

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.280
numbers year over year.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.180
Considering this, Netflix’s subscriber base
is at the very least stagnating.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.040
And given Netflix estimates that they will
lose another 2 million subscribers this quarter,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.320
this lack luster performance doesn’t seem
to be a one off anomaly either.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.740
So, what’s causing people to leave in droves?

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.370
Well, the answer is quite straightforward.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:29.370
The primary reason that people cancel subscriptions
is because they feel that its no longer worthwhile.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.941
For the longest time, Netflix has not only
been losing some of their best content, but

00:03:33.941 --> 00:03:36.210
they have been constantly increasing prices.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:41.790
Currently, Netflix charges $9.99 for the basic
plan or $120 per year.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:47.710
Meanwhile, Disney + costs $8 per month and
they have annual plan that costs $80 per year.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:52.510
So, by getting a Disney+ subscription, viewers
can not only save up to 50% on subscription

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:56.060
fees, but they can arguably get access to
better content.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.260
While Netflix does still have a large lead
in terms of total movies and TV shows against

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.540
Disney, this doesn’t really mean much given
that the average person doesn’t care about

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.380
the vast majority of the content on Netflix.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.510
Conversely, while Disney+ has a smaller catalog,
everything they do have is bangers from Star

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.590
Wars to the Avengers.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.350
Something else to note is that it’s not
like Netflix had to make this price increments

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:19.400
either.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.510
Much of their price increases over the past
6 years have gone directly to their bottom

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:24.510
line.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:30.010
Since 2016, Netflix’s net margin has exploded
from about 2% to 17%.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:34.470
And it seems that as Netflix’s profits went
parabolic, their value proposition fell off

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.380
a cliff.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:41.100
RAY OF HOPE:
While it seems like Netflix is just going

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.130
to continue losing content to competitors
as production studios open up their own streaming

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.350
services, there is some hope for Netflix.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:52.650
After I posted my last video on Netflix, a
subscriber actually reached out to me and

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:56.850
let me know Netflix actually has a lot of
background licensing deals that have yet to

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:57.850
come to fruition.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:02.130
For example, Netflix apparently has a deal
with Disney to license all of the movies that

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.260
came out between January of 2016 and December
of 2018.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:11.380
The catch, however, is that Netflix can’t
start streaming these movies until 2026.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:15.620
This gives Disney 4 more years to eat into
Netflix’s customer base.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:21.350
And given that Disney+ already has 129 million
subscribers and that Disney+ has only been

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.790
a thing for a little more than 2 years, this
doesn’t bode well for Netflix.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:31.010
Aside from having plenty of time to steal
customers from Netflix, Disney will also has

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:36.910
8 years worth of backlog content between 2018
and 2026 that Netflix wont have access to.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:42.110
And I doubt that Disney will be making any
such deals with Netflix moving forward.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.440
This applies to all the other production studios
as well.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.490
While they may be tied up with Netflix for
several more years, you can bet that they

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.810
won’t be extending such deals much further.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:57.639
So, really, the only solution Netflix has
for this conundrum is to make their own content,

00:05:57.639 --> 00:06:01.020
and this is fortunately exactly what they’ve
been doing.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.920
But, the problem is that most of their originals
are hit or miss.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.670
While you have some mega hits like Squid Game,
the vast majority of their original shows

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.360
don’t receive much fanfare.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.130
Now, this isn’t exactly Netflix’s fault.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.780
It’s simply the nature of the media business.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:19.940
You often have to swing several times to hit
a home run.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:25.810
The problem though is this process is not
only capital intensive but time intensive.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.820
Production studios like Disney, Warner Brothers,
and Paramount Studios have literally been

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.840
swinging for over a hundred years to produce
the classics that we all know and love today.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.900
Meanwhile, Netflix has only been swinging
for 10 years.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.510
So, it’s no wonder that they’ve been struggling
to build up the catalog that their competitors

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.510
have.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:46.850
Something else to note is that Netflix’s
streaming model has inadvertently made it

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.320
a lot harder for them to retain customers.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.410
Generally, whenever Netflix launches a new
season of a show, they post the entire season

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:54.430
right away.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:57.820
Now, I’m a massive fan of this approach.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:02.280
Being able to simply press next episode after
a crazy cliff hanger is so nice.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.550
But, from a business perspective, this is
not that great.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:10.990
Over the years, Netflix has created a phenomenon
that a lot of us are extremely familiar with

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:12.240
and that’s binging.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.430
The best bingers amongst us are usually done
with a season by 5 am the next morning.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:21.900
But, even if you’re not that hardcore, you’re
likely done within a week.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.390
With regular TV, you would’ve had to spend
months if not years watching the same show.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:30.552
And the opportunity to binge has made it extremely
convenient for customers to buy a netflix

00:07:30.552 --> 00:07:33.670
subscription for a month and then instantly
cancel.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:38.889
And as you would guess, this is not that great
for customer retention or long term profits.

00:07:38.889 --> 00:07:44.950
A NEW UNDERDOG:
It’s no secret that over the years, our

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:49.860
attention spans have been continuously decreasing
and the best evidence of this is the rise

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:50.980
of TikTok.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:56.440
Initially, this was actually one of the main
reasons that Netflix even did so well.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:00.600
People didn’t have to wait months or years
to watch a show, they could binge over a few

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.600
weeks.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.480
But, the problem is that the average person’s
patience and attention has shrunk even smaller

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.940
and Netflix has struggled to keep up.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.740
One of the worst parts of watching Netflix
is trying to find something good.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:17.639
Oftentimes, I spend 20 to 30 minutes just
scrolling through Netflix and looking up reviews

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:21.230
until I finally find something to watch, and
I’m sure a lot of you can relate to this.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:24.560
Meanwhile, YouTube and TikTok have perfected
their content recommendation algorithms which

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.550
give us an endless stream of content to watch.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:32.589
Statistically speaking, 82% of you guys didn’t
search up this video, rather, it was recommended

00:08:32.589 --> 00:08:33.589
to you.

00:08:33.589 --> 00:08:37.010
Only 5.6% of you guys actually looked up this
video.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:41.060
And while, the YouTube search box is still
super helpful if you’re trying to find a

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:45.520
tutorial or a review about a certain product,
for casual entertainment, refreshing the home

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.400
page until you find something good to watch
is usually the way to go.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:54.120
With Netflix, however, you can’t really
do this, and this brings up a serious question.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.130
Is it actaully Disney+ and Hulu and Paramount+
that are stealing Netflix’s watch time?

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:03.580
Or is it YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, and SnapChat.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:08.070
About 10 years ago, we had a very similar
situation with Netflix and traditional TV.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.710
TV channels were bleeding watch time like
crazy, but it wasn’t other TV channels that

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:14.020
were stealing it from them.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.170
Rather, the culprit was Netflix.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:20.510
Similarly, I believe the culprit today is
actually social media.

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:24.330
After all, TikTok was literally the most visited
site in 2021.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:29.240
So, Netflix is not only competing against
production companies but also social media

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:30.240
companies.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.480
And while Netflix may have a chance against
the production companies, I’m not as optimistic

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.290
when it comes to their chances against social
media.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:42.650
HISTORICAL COMPS:
By now, I think we’ve made it pretty clear

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:45.630
that Netflix isn’t doing that great fundamentally.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:50.320
This isn’t to say that Netflix is gonna
file for bankruptcy anytime soon, but it does

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.380
look like they’ve hit a local top.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:57.050
And the question is: can Netflix adapt to
changing times and keep this a local top or

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.430
will this become an absolute top.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:03.670
Well, to answer this question, we can take
a look at historical charts of other companies

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:05.930
that underwent a similar crisis like Microsoft.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:11.930
Throughout the 80s and 90s, Microsoft crushed
it as personal computer adoption went to the

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:12.930
moon.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:17.350
But, by the early 2000s, Microsoft hit market
saturation and this is also coincided with

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:20.290
the dotcom crash which left Microsoft devastated.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:25.760
During the dotcom crash, Microsoft shed a
total of 66% which is quite similar to Netflix

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:26.760
today.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:32.740
The thing to note though is that it took Microsoft
16 years to recover and make new all time

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:33.740
highs.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:37.890
Now, if Microsoft had embraced the smartphone
boom, I think they could’ve made this recovery

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:41.820
much sooner maybe in 2009 or 2010.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:45.830
But they didn’t, and it wasn’t until the
embraced the cloud computing boom that they

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:46.830
finally took off again.

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:51.320
Now, with Netflix, I think they’re already
too late to really capitalize on the social

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:56.040
media/user generated content boom, so like
Microsoft, they’re gonna have to turn their

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.230
attention onto the next big thing.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:05.010
Maybe, that’s VR and AR content, I’m not
sure, but one thing I am sure of is that making

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.830
such a transition will take quite a bit of
time.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:11.500
Combine this with the fed tightening and a
great deleveraging potentially around the

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:15.020
corner, and Netflix may not make new all time
highs for years to come.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:19.470
Also, let’s not forget that there is no
guarantee that Netflix must make a new all

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:21.660
time high in the first place.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:26.300
Given their current situation, they could
easily end up like Cisco or Intel.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:32.590
CAN NETFLIX RECOVER:
While this video has been quite critical on

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:37.140
Netflix, I’m still leaning on the side of
betting that Netflix will eventually recover.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.900
Even though their user count is stagnating,
I don’t think their innovative spirit is

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:45.042
stagnating, and I’m rather confident that
Reed Hastings will figure something out by

00:11:45.042 --> 00:11:47.370
opening up Netflix to a new market.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:51.459
With all of that being said though, this type
of transition wont take place within a matter

00:11:51.459 --> 00:11:53.250
of 6 or 12 months.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:57.780
For a mega cap company like Netflix to pivot
into new sectors and make these endeavors

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.630
profitable, it’ll take several years.

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:04.850
And in the meantime, investing in Netflix
is really just a toss up.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:10.140
Given how oversold the stock is, we may see
a recovery back up to $400 or $500.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:14.200
On the other hand, if the market really starts
to sell off as the fed tightens, there may

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.980
be a lot more pain to come for Netflix.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:21.270
Either way though, for Netflix to become the
fundamental king that they were in the 2010s,

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:24.709
I think it’s gonna take several years of
trial and error.

00:12:24.709 --> 00:12:28.550
And personally, I wouldn’t invest serious
capital into the company until they figure

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:30.250
out another mode of growth.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.290
But that’s just what I think.

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:33.880
Would you guys invest in Netflix?

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.880
Comment that down below.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.550
Also, drop a like if you’d rather watch
YouTube than Netflix.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:43.190
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages.

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:49.170
And consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

